 "usage": "Give oops = the kernel's text (from the first report line through 'end trace'); arch = auto unless detection is wrong; code = a hex ESR/#PF/FSR/scause to decode instead of the log's. It returns the kind, faulting function, fault address, access type, CPU/PID/comm, kernel version, the call stack table (faulting frame first, unreliable and fault-path frames marked), the registers holding NULL / the fault address / the base of the faulting access, the fault syndrome fields, the taint flags, and texts: Commands (decode_stacktrace.sh, faddr2line, gdb, decodecode), Summary and the decoded Code: line. Use it to triage a crash log before opening the source.",
 "sources": [
  "Linux Documentation/admin-guide/tainted-kernels.rst (taint letters and bits)",
  "Linux Documentation/admin-guide/bug-hunting.rst and scripts/decode_stacktrace.sh, scripts/faddr2line, scripts/decodecode",
  "Arm Architecture Reference Manual for A-profile, ESR_EL1 (EC, ISS for data/instruction aborts, DFSC/IFSC); arch/arm64/include/asm/esr.h, brk-imm.h; esr_class_str[] in arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c",
  "Arm ARM A64 load/store encodings (LDR/STR immediate, LDUR, LDP/STP) for the Code: line",
  "arch/x86/include/asm/trap_pf.h (page fault error code bits)",
  "arch/arm/mm/fsr-2level.c fsr_info[] (ARM short-descriptor FSR)",
  "RISC-V Privileged Architecture v1.12, Table 4.2 (scause exception codes)"
 ],
 "agentOmit": [
  "draw"
 ]
}
